*Section 2:*
The 17th century witnessed the mechanization of calculation, driven by mathematicians who sought to eliminate arithmetic errors. Blaise Pascal, born in 1623, developed the Pascaline in 1642, which was the first mechanical calculator capable of addition and subtraction. It utilized a series of geared wheels, each representing a decimal digit, and used a carry mechanism to propagate sums to the next wheel. While functional, it was a one-off device. The limitations of manufacturing in the 17th century meant that such devices were expensive and difficult to reproduce. By the 1690s, Leibniz had improved upon Pascal's design with the Stepped Reckoner. This machine introduced the concept of the "step" mechanism, which allowed for multiplication and division by reducing the problem to repeated addition. However, the Stepped Reckoner was fragile and required intricate manual operation.

Charles Babbage, working in the 1830s, envisioned the Analytical Engine, a far more ambitious design than Pascal's. His Difference Engine was intended to compute polynomial values automatically using punched cards. The engine used gears to move a carriage along a paper tape, printing the results. The key innovation here was the separation of the calculation engine from the programming mechanism. The punched card served as the program. However, the Difference Engine stalled for reasons that were a combination of technical and economic. The manufacturing tolerances of the industrial revolution were insufficient for the high-precision gears required. A single gear with an error of a fraction of a millimeter could cause the machine to jam. Furthermore, Babbage's funding was inconsistent, and his health was failing. He died in 1871 before his machines were completed. The Analytical Engine, designed in the 1830s, was a general-purpose computer in design but remained unfinished in execution. The gap between the theoretical design and the physical realization highlighted the difficulty of building a computer without a stored-program architecture. The Difference Engine stalled because the gears had to be built to exact specifications that were impossible to achieve with the tools available at the time. The mechanical era was limited by the physics of friction, wear, and the cost of precision.

